---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import FractionCalculator from '@/components/tools/math/FractionCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="fraction-calculator">
  <FractionCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the fraction calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type the first value. Whole numbers (<code>5</code>), simple fractions (<code>7/8</code>), mixed numbers (<code>2 3/4</code>) and decimals (<code>1.25</code>) are all accepted.</li>
      <li>Choose add, subtract, multiply or divide.</li>
      <li>Type the second value the same way.</li>
      <li>Read the answer as a simplified fraction, a mixed number, a decimal and a percentage, with each step of the working shown below.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Put the minus sign in front of the whole value: <code>-2 1/2</code> and <code>-7/8</code> both work.
      The calculator stores fractions as pairs of arbitrary-precision integers, so results are exact, with no
      floating-point rounding.
    </p>

    <h2>The rules, in the order the calculator applies them</h2>
    <h3>Adding and subtracting</h3>
    <p>
      You can add fractions only when the pieces are the same size, so both fractions first get a common
      denominator. The calculator uses the least common denominator (the LCM of the two denominators),
      which keeps the numbers small and usually removes the need to simplify later.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, 2 3/4 + 5/6 becomes 11/4 + 5/6. The LCM of 4 and 6 is 12, so the fractions become 33/12
      and 10/12, which give 43/12, or 3 7/12.
    </p>
    <h3>Multiplying</h3>
    <p>
      Multiply straight across: numerator by numerator, denominator by denominator. No common denominator is
      necessary. Shared factors between the top and bottom of the product are cancelled at the end.
    </p>
    <h3>Dividing</h3>
    <p>
      Dividing by a fraction is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal: invert the second fraction and
      change the ÷ into a ×. This works because dividing by 3/4 asks “how many three-quarters fit in here?”,
      which is the same as multiplying by 4/3.
    </p>
    <h3>Simplifying</h3>
    <p>
      A fraction is in lowest terms when the numerator and denominator have no common factor. Dividing both
      by their greatest common divisor does this in one step, as the last line of the working shows.
    </p>

    <h2>Common fraction ↔ decimal ↔ percent equivalents</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Fraction</th>
            <th scope="col">Decimal</th>
            <th scope="col">Percent</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1/2</td><td>0.5</td><td>50%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/3</td><td>0.333…</td><td>33.33%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2/3</td><td>0.666…</td><td>66.67%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/4</td><td>0.25</td><td>25%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3/4</td><td>0.75</td><td>75%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/5</td><td>0.2</td><td>20%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/6</td><td>0.1666…</td><td>16.67%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/8</td><td>0.125</td><td>12.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3/8</td><td>0.375</td><td>37.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/10</td><td>0.1</td><td>10%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/16</td><td>0.0625</td><td>6.25%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Where fraction arithmetic actually comes up</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Cooking: half of a recipe that needs 2/3 cup is 2/3 ÷ 2 = 1/3.</li>
      <li>Woodwork and construction: imperial measurements use sixteenths of an inch, and 3/16 plus 5/8 needs a common denominator.</li>
      <li>Music: note lengths are fractions of a bar, and a bar must add up exactly.</li>
      <li>Probability: combining independent events multiplies fractions directly.</li>
      <li>Homework: the working matters as much as the answer, so each step is listed and can be copied.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why exact arithmetic matters here</h2>
    <p>
      Some fraction tools convert to a decimal, calculate, then estimate a fraction from the result. This
      gives answers such as 0.3333333333 for 1/3, and 1/3 + 1/6 can come out slightly different from 1/2.
      This calculator uses only integer arithmetic: it keeps the numerator and denominator as big integers,
      reduces by the greatest common divisor, and converts to a decimal only at the end, for display. All
      calculations run on your device, and nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
